    name: "APUSH DBQ on the Erie Canal, no blank lines",
    expect: "dbq",
    text: `Kayla Brennan
APUSH – Unit 4 DBQ
Prompt: Evaluate the extent to which the Erie Canal transformed the economy of the northern United States between 1817 and 1850.
In the early 1800s, moving goods across the Appalachian Mountains was slow and expensive, and western farmers mostly sent their crops down the Mississippi to New Orleans. When New York began digging a canal from Albany to Buffalo in 1817, critics called it "Clinton's Ditch." By the time it opened in 1825, however, it had begun to reorganize the economy of the North around east–west trade. The Erie Canal transformed the northern economy to a great extent by lowering transportation costs, creating new cities, and tying western agriculture to eastern markets, although its effects were uneven and were soon challenged by railroads.
The canal's most immediate effect was on the cost of shipping. Governor DeWitt Clinton argued before construction that a canal would make New York City "the granary of the world" and the center of the nation's commerce (Doc 1). Clinton was trying to win money from a skeptical legislature, so he had reason to exaggerate, but the freight rates in Doc 2 suggest he was not far off. The cost of moving a ton of goods from Buffalo to New York City fell from roughly $100 by wagon to around $10 by canal boat, and the trip took days instead of weeks (Doc 2). Lower costs meant that bulky goods like flour, lumber and potash could now be sold profitably in the East.
These new trade routes created boomtowns along the canal. Rochester, a village of about 1,500 people in 1820, became a city of more than 30,000 by 1850, largely because its mills could ship flour by water (Doc 4). A British traveler in 1831 described Rochester's streets as crowded with warehouses and mills and its canal basin as full of boats loading at all hours (Doc 3). Because she was a foreign visitor writing for readers back home, she may have emphasized how new and busy American towns seemed, but the population figures support her impression. Buffalo grew in the same way, becoming the transfer point between Great Lakes ships and canal boats.
The canal also tied the Old Northwest to the Northeast. A farmer in Ohio wrote to his brother in 1838 that he could finally get a fair price for wheat because buyers shipped it east through Cleveland and the canal (Doc 6). Ohio built its own canals connecting to Lake Erie, and this east–west link helped create a market economy in which western farmers specialized in grain while eastern cities produced manufactured goods. Historians have argued that this link pulled the Old Northwest economically toward the North rather than the South, which mattered politically by the 1850s.
However, the canal's transformation was not complete or permanent. Irish immigrant laborers who dug much of the canal worked for low wages in dangerous conditions, and an 1826 newspaper editorial complained about the rowdy crowds that canal traffic brought to small towns (Doc 5). The benefits of the new economy went mostly to merchants and mill owners. In addition, by the 1840s railroads were already competing with the canal, and a cartoon from 1849 shows a locomotive racing past a mule-drawn boat (Doc 7). Other states rushed to copy New York's success, and Pennsylvania's Main Line canal system lost money for years.
In conclusion, the Erie Canal transformed the northern economy to a large extent by cutting shipping costs, creating cities like Rochester and Buffalo, and linking western farms to eastern markets. Although its workers did not share equally in its profits and railroads would eventually overtake it, the canal helped make New York City the nation's leading port and laid the foundation for the market revolution.`,
