    name: "Middle-school DBQ on how the Inca held their empire together",
    expect: "dbq",
    text: `Name: Jaylen Rivera
Ms. Okafor – Social Studies, Period 3
DBQ: How Did the Inca Hold Their Empire Together?

Imagine trying to rule ten million people spread across mountains, deserts and jungles without horses, wheels or a written alphabet. That was the challenge facing the Inca in the 1400s and early 1500s. Their empire, called Tawantinsuyu, stretched about 2,500 miles along the Andes. The Inca held their empire together by building roads, keeping careful records, and making conquered people work and speak like Inca.

My first reason is roads. According to Document A, the Inca built about 25,000 miles of roads, including rope bridges across deep canyons. Relay runners called chasquis could carry a message about 150 miles in a single day (Doc. A). This shows that the emperor in Cusco could find out about a rebellion quickly and send soldiers. Document B also shows tambos, or storehouses, along the roads where armies and travelers could get food. Roads helped the Inca hold their empire together because they connected the far parts of it to the capital.

My second reason is record keeping. In Document C, a Spanish writer describes the quipu, a set of knotted strings that Inca officials used to count people, llamas and crops. Different colors and knots stood for different things. Even though the Inca did not have writing, the quipu let them know exactly how many workers each village had and how much food was stored (Document C). If you know what everyone has, it is easier to collect taxes and plan for famines.

My third reason is that the Inca made conquered people part of the empire. Document D explains the mit'a, a labor tax where every family had to send workers to build roads, farm, or serve in the army for part of the year. The Inca also made everyone learn Quechua, their language, and sometimes moved whole villages of loyal people into newly conquered areas (Doc. D). This made it harder for people to rebel because they were mixed together and had to talk to each other in the same language.

Some people might say the Inca empire did not stay together, because it fell to the Spanish in 1532. But the empire was already weakened by a civil war between two brothers, Atahualpa and Huáscar, and by smallpox that spread before Pizarro even arrived. For about a hundred years the system worked.

In conclusion, the Inca held their huge empire together with roads, quipus, and the mit'a and Quechua language. Without these, the empire would have broken apart much sooner.`,
