    name: "Short story told entirely in dialogue",
    expect: "story",
    text: `Last Train

"Is this seat taken?"
"It's the last train. Every seat is taken by somebody who isn't here."
"That's very poetic for one in the morning."
"I'm a very poetic conductor. Ticket?"
"I don't have one. The machine was broken."
"The machine is always broken. That's how I know you're telling the truth. Where are you going?"
"Wherever this ends."
"It ends at Halvard Point. There's nothing at Halvard Point but the sea and a bakery that opens at five."
"Then I'll wait for the bakery."
"You're not running from anyone, are you?"
"Only a wedding."
"Yours?"
"It was supposed to be."
"Ah. And the groom?"
"Is probably still standing by the cake, explaining to my mother that I needed some air."
"That's a lot of air. Halvard Point is ninety miles."
"I needed a lot of air."
"Well. The rolls with cardamom are the good ones. Tell Agnes I sent you, and she'll give you two."
"Why would she do that?"
"Because forty years ago I was the one on this train in a suit, and she was the one who sold me a roll and told me to go back."
"Did you?"
"No. I married her instead."
"So you think I should go back?"
"I think you should eat something first. Next stop, Halvard Point."`,
