    name: "Middle-school fantasy story with a byline",
    expect: "story",
    text: `The Clockmaker's Apprentice
By Arjun Mehta

Lena had always been told not to touch the clock in the back of her grandfather's shop. It was taller than she was, with a face painted with moons and a pendulum shaped like a fish. It had not ticked in fifty years.

One rainy Saturday, while her grandfather was at the hardware store, Lena noticed something strange. The fish pendulum was moving, just a little, back and forth. She stepped closer. There was a tiny brass door under the clock face that she had never seen before, and it was open a crack.

"Hello?" she whispered, feeling silly.

A small voice answered. "Finally. Do you know how long I've been waiting for someone to notice me?"

Lena jumped back and knocked over a box of gears. Peeking out of the brass door was a mouse wearing a pair of tiny spectacles.

"I'm Tobias," the mouse said. "I keep this clock running. Or I did, until someone lost the key."

"What key?" Lena asked.

"The winding key. Without it, the clock can't run, and without the clock, I can't get home." Tobias pointed a paw at the moons painted on the clock face. "I come from there, you see."

Lena spent the whole afternoon searching. She looked in drawers full of springs and jars full of screws. Finally, she remembered the old photo on her grandfather's desk. In it, he was young and holding a key on a red ribbon. She opened the desk's bottom drawer, and there it was, still tied to the same ribbon.

She fit the key into the clock and turned it three times. The pendulum swung, the moons began to glow, and Tobias tipped his spectacles.

"Thank you, Lena," he said. "Tell your grandfather I said hello. He was my last apprentice."

Then the brass door clicked shut.

When her grandfather came home, the clock was ticking for the first time in fifty years. He looked at it for a long time. Then he looked at Lena and smiled like he already knew.`,
    why: "Third-person past-tense children's fantasy with a title, byline, dialogue and a plot arc.",
