    name: "Eulogy for a grandfather",
    expect: "speech",
    text: `Eulogy for Ernesto Salgado
Delivered at St. Brigid's, Saturday, March 7

Good morning. For those who don't know me, I'm Lucia, Ernesto's oldest granddaughter. On behalf of my grandmother and our whole family, thank you all for being here. I know many of you drove a long way, and Abuelo would have been embarrassed by the fuss, and secretly very pleased.

When my family asked me to speak today, I tried to think of the one thing that captured my grandfather. It wasn't hard. It was his hands. They were always busy. For thirty-four years those hands fixed the elevators in half the buildings downtown. If you've ridden an elevator in this city, there's a good chance he once lay on his back underneath it with a flashlight in his mouth.

At home, those same hands made tortillas every Sunday, badly. He never got the circles right. He said the shape didn't matter as long as they were warm, and he was right.

His hands taught me how to ride a bike, how to change a tire, and how to shake someone's hand like I meant it. When I was nine and failed my first spelling test, he didn't say anything. He just took my hand and walked me to the corner store and bought me a lemon ice, and we sat on the curb until I stopped crying. Then he said, "Now we study." And we did, every night for a month.

Some of you knew him as a coworker, some as a neighbor, some as the man at the 8 a.m. Mass who sang a half step flat and didn't care. I see people here from his union, from the parish, from the block association. He would want me to tell you that he valued every one of you, even if he showed it by fixing your gate instead of saying so.

Abuelo, thank you for your patience, for your terrible tortillas, and for your hands, which held all of us up. We'll take it from here.

Thank you.`,
