    name: "Regional news story on island ferry fares, no blank lines",
    expect: "news",
    text: `Island ferry fares to rise 18 percent despite residents' objections
By Colin Darrow, Staff Writer
HALLETT ISLAND, Maine — Round-trip ferry fares between Hallett Island and the mainland will rise 18 percent beginning Jan. 1 after the state transportation board voted 4-1 on Thursday to approve the increase, ending months of debate over how to close a growing gap in the ferry service's budget.
The fare for an adult passenger will go from $22 to $26, and the fare for a car and driver will go from $68 to $80. Fares for island residents with year-round passes will rise by 12 percent.
Board members said the increase was needed to cover rising fuel and insurance costs and the price of replacing the service's oldest vessel, the Margaret B., which was built in 1979. "Nobody on this board wanted to raise fares," said board chair Diane Kessler. "But the alternative was cutting trips, and for people on the island, the boat is the road."
More than 150 people attended a public hearing on the proposal in October, and most of the 40 who spoke opposed it. Several residents said the increase would fall hardest on older islanders who travel to the mainland for medical appointments and on fishermen who must haul gear and supplies.
"I go across twice a week for dialysis," said Ruth Ames, 74, who has lived on the island her whole life. "That's another few hundred dollars a year I don't have."
The board's lone dissenting vote came from member Paul Theriault, who argued that the state should cover the shortfall from its general transportation fund. "We don't charge people a toll to drive on Route 1," Theriault said. "Islanders shouldn't pay more to get to the same hospital."
Under the approved plan, the board will also create a reduced fare for residents 65 and older and for travelers with medical appointments, though details have not been finalized. Kessler said the board expects to vote on that program in December.
The island has about 1,100 year-round residents, a number that more than triples in the summer. The ferry carried roughly 210,000 passengers last year, according to state figures.`,
