    name: "Common App essay about moth-watching, no blank lines",
    expect: "personal",
    text: `Common App Essay – Prompt 6 (draft 3)
On Friday nights in July, while my friends are at the movies, I am standing in my backyard in the dark, staring at a bedsheet.
The sheet is white, pinned to the clothesline, with a black-light bulb clamped above it. By ten o'clock it is covered with moths: tiny brown ones no bigger than a fingernail, green ones the color of new leaves, and, if I am lucky, a sphinx moth as big as my palm, its wings patterned like tree bark. I photograph each one, upload it to a citizen science database, and try to figure out its name.
I started moth-watching the summer I was fourteen, almost by accident. My younger brother had a school project on insects and needed a "night specimen," so we hung a sheet and waited. He lost interest after twenty minutes. I stayed out until one in the morning. I had expected maybe a dozen moths. Instead, I counted more than sixty, at least twenty different kinds, in a yard I thought I knew completely.
That night changed how I look at ordinary places. Before, I thought of my neighborhood as boring: a cul-de-sac of identical houses, a strip mall, a drainage pond. Now I know that the drainage pond supports a moth called the American lotus borer, and that the oak tree in the Hendersons' front yard feeds caterpillars of at least six species. Our suburb has a hidden night shift, and almost nobody sees it.
Moths also taught me patience, which does not come naturally to me. I am the kind of person who reads the last page of a book first. But moths do not show up on schedule. Some nights the sheet stays empty for an hour, and then, at 11:40, it is suddenly crowded. I have learned to wait without checking my phone, to sit in the dark and listen to the crickets, and to accept that the most interesting thing might arrive after I would normally have given up.
Most of all, moths taught me how much I do not know. The first time I submitted a photo, I labeled it confidently as a "brown moth." An entomologist in Ohio I had never met kindly corrected me: it was a Clymene moth, cream-colored with a dark cross-shaped marking, and I had photographed it in bad light. Since then, experts I will never meet in person have corrected my identifications dozens of times. At first this was embarrassing. Now it is the part I like best. Every correction means I see a little more clearly next time.
Last summer, I recorded a species that had never been documented in our county before, a small orange moth called the scarlet-winged lichen moth. When the database confirmed it, I ran inside and woke up my brother, who mumbled "cool" and went back to sleep. It did not matter. I knew that the map of what lives here was slightly more accurate because I had been paying attention.
I want to study ecology because I want to keep doing what the bedsheet taught me to do: look closely at places that seem ordinary, wait longer than is comfortable, and stay open to being wrong. I do not know yet whether I will study moths, or soil, or rivers. But I know that wherever I end up, I will be the person who notices what comes out after dark.`,
    why: "College application essay about the writer's own hobby and growth, with a prompt line and no blank lines; science vocabulary but no lab structure.",
