    name: "Sixth-grade reflection on a science fair project",
    expect: "personal",
    text: `Science Fair Reflection
Name: Mateo Gutierrez
Grade 6, Ms. Lindgren

When I started my science fair project, I thought it would be easy. My question was whether music makes plants grow faster. I put three bean plants in my room. One got classical music, one got rock music, and one got no music. I was sure the classical plant would win because I read that online.

The hardest part was that nothing happened for a really long time. I checked my plants every day for the first week and they were just dirt. I almost gave up and told my mom I wanted to change my project. She said scientists have to be patient, so I kept going.

Then my cat knocked over the rock music plant. I was really upset because I thought my whole project was ruined. My teacher told me that this happens to real scientists too and that I should write about it as an error. That made me feel better. I replanted it, but it was behind the others for the rest of the project.

In the end, my results didn't really show that music helps plants grow. All of them were about the same height except the one the cat knocked over. At first I was disappointed that my hypothesis was wrong. But now I think that it is okay to be wrong, as long as you are honest about it.

If I did it again, I would use more plants and keep them somewhere the cat can't reach. I also learned that I like doing experiments more than I thought I would. Next year I want to do a project about weather.`,
