    expect: "annotated",
    text: `Liam Sorensen
Ms. Patel
U.S. History, Period 4
2 February 2026
Annotated Bibliography: The Great Molasses Flood of 1919
Beckett, Harold. Sticky Disaster: Boston and the Molasses Tank Collapse. Commonwealth Books, 2012.
This book tells the full story of the January 15, 1919, collapse of a 50-foot molasses tank in Boston's North End, which released more than two million gallons of molasses and killed 21 people. Beckett explains how the tank was built quickly and never properly tested, and how the company ignored complaints that it leaked. The book is well researched and uses court records and newspaper reports. I will use it as my main source for the events of the disaster and the lawsuit that followed.
Doyle, Maureen. "Why Molasses Moves So Fast." Science in History, 14 Jan. 2019.
This online article explains the science behind the disaster, including how cold air made the molasses thicker after it spread, which trapped victims and made rescue difficult. The author interviews a physicist who studied the flood using computer models. It is helpful for understanding why the wave was so deadly, but it is short and does not include citations, so I will check its facts against other sources.
Kessler, Paul, editor. The North End: An Immigrant Neighborhood, 1880–1930. Harbor Street Press, 2008.
This collection of essays describes the Italian and Irish immigrant community where the disaster happened. One chapter discusses how the company tried to blame anarchists for the collapse and how residents felt the company had treated their neighborhood carelessly because it was poor and immigrant. This source will help me explain the social background of the flood and why it mattered beyond the deaths.
"Molasses Tank Bursts in North End." Boston Daily Ledger, 15 Jan. 1919, p. 1.
This front-page newspaper article was written on the day of the disaster, before the full death toll was known. It describes the scene from eyewitnesses, including the sound of the tank bursting and horses trapped in the molasses. As a primary source, it shows how people understood the event at the time. However, some of its details, like the number of deaths, were wrong, so I will use it only for eyewitness descriptions.`,
