    name: "Compare/contrast essay on two bridges with Works Cited",
    expect: "prose",
    text: `Shannon Kowalski
Honors English 9
Mrs. Delacroix
February 20, 2026

Two Bridges, Two Eras: The Brooklyn Bridge and the Golden Gate Bridge

The Brooklyn Bridge and the Golden Gate Bridge are two of the most famous bridges in the world, and both have become symbols of the cities they serve. Both are suspension bridges that were considered impossible when they were first proposed. However, they were built more than fifty years apart, and the differences between them show how much engineering, safety and design changed between the 1870s and the 1930s.

The two bridges have a lot in common. Each one solved a serious transportation problem. Before the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, the only way to travel between Manhattan and Brooklyn was by ferry, which was slow and dangerous in winter (Hartwell 34). Similarly, before the Golden Gate Bridge opened in 1937, people in Marin County had to take a ferry to reach San Francisco (Osei 45). Both bridges also faced strong opposition. Critics said the East River was too wide and the Golden Gate strait was too windy and foggy for a bridge to be safe. In both cases, a determined engineer refused to give up.

However, the bridges are very different in their materials and appearance. The Brooklyn Bridge combines steel cables with massive towers made of granite and limestone, built with pointed Gothic arches that make it look almost like a cathedral (Hartwell 52). The Golden Gate Bridge, in contrast, is made almost entirely of steel, with Art Deco towers and an orange-red color called International Orange. Its color was chosen partly so that it would stay visible in the fog (Osei 47). The Golden Gate is also much larger. Its main span is 4,200 feet, more than twice as long as the Brooklyn Bridge's span of about 1,600 feet, and it was the longest suspension span in the world when it opened (Osei 46).

The biggest difference between the two bridges is the way they treated their workers. The Brooklyn Bridge was dangerous to build. Workers dug the foundations inside caissons, huge boxes sunk to the bottom of the river, and many suffered from decompression sickness, which people called "caisson disease." Even the chief engineer, Washington Roebling, became too sick to visit the site, and his wife, Emily Warren Roebling, carried his instructions to the workers and became an expert in engineering herself (Hartwell 71). By the 1930s, safety had improved. The Golden Gate's chief engineer, Joseph Strauss, required hard hats and installed a large safety net under the bridge, which saved the lives of nineteen men (Osei 49). Still, eleven workers died during construction, ten of them in a single accident when a platform broke through the net.

In conclusion, the Brooklyn Bridge and the Golden Gate Bridge are both remarkable achievements that connected their cities and proved critics wrong. Yet the differences between them, from stone towers to steel ones and from caisson disease to safety nets, show how engineering grew safer and more ambitious in the fifty years between them. Together, they tell the story of how America learned to build bigger and better.

Works Cited
Hartwell, Simone. Spans of Steel: American Bridges, 1870–1940. Meridian Press, 2015.
Osei, Daniel. "Orange by Accident: The Making of the Golden Gate." Bay Area Heritage Quarterly, vol. 12, no. 3, 2019, pp. 44–51.`,
    why: "Five-paragraph compare/contrast essay with in-text citations and a two-entry Works Cited; the citations alone do not make it a research paper.",
